/*!
\page page_trivial_functions Trivial functions
\tableofcontents

A trivial function is a C API function that only reads or writes a value that is already in memory.
It does no other work.
The reference documentation marks each trivial function with a "Trivial function" note.
The \ref trivial "list of trivial functions" shows all of them.

\section section_trivial_guarantees Guarantees

While a trivial function runs, it:

<ul>
<li>does not acquire a lock and does not wait for another thread,
<li>does not allocate or free memory,
<li>does not raise an internal C++ exception,
<li>does not write to the log, so it never calls the callback set with \ref Logging::Logging_SetCallbackLogger,
<li>does not call any other callback that the application supplied,
<li>does not read files or streams, and does not parse, decode or encode data,
<li>does not change the last error of the thread, see below,
<li>returns after a small, fixed amount of work.
</ul>

\section section_trivial_errors Error reporting

A trivial function can still fail. It then returns an error code, for example:

<ul>
<li>\ref VANILLAPDF_ERROR_PARAMETER_VALUE when a handle or an output pointer is \b NULL, or when a conversion such as \ref ButtonFieldHandle::ButtonField_FromField gets an object of a different type,
<li>\ref VANILLAPDF_ERROR_GENERAL when an input value is not a member of the enumeration.
</ul>

A trivial function does not store an error message.
After it fails, \ref Errors::Errors_GetLastError and \ref Errors::Errors_GetLastErrorMessage still return the values of the last function that stored an error.
Use the return code of the trivial function.

\section section_trivial_limits What trivial does not mean

<ul>
<li>A trivial function adds no synchronization.
It is safe on an object that no other thread changes at the same time.
If another thread calls a setter on the same object at the same time, the result is a data race, as with every other function.
<li>A trivial function does not check that a handle is still valid.
A handle that was already released causes undefined behavior, as with every other function.
<li>Not every getter is trivial.
Many getters take a lock (for example on dictionaries, streams and strings) or load data on demand.
Only the functions with the "Trivial function" note keep this contract.
</ul>

\section section_trivial_bindings Use in language bindings

The contract lets a language binding call these functions in a cheaper way.
For example, .NET can call a native function with the <tt>[SuppressGCTransition]</tt> attribute.
The runtime then does not switch the thread from managed to native mode, which saves a few nanoseconds per call.
This is only safe when the native function returns quickly, never blocks and never calls back into managed code.
The trivial function contract gives these guarantees.
The Vanilla.PDF .NET binding uses this attribute for the trivial functions.

\section section_trivial_stability Stability

The "Trivial function" note is part of the API contract.
A function that has the note keeps it in all later versions with the same major version.
New functions can get the note in a minor version.

*/
